			],
			"Message": "style(Output): Normalize esbuild output with imports-first ordering and tab indentation\n\nRe-align all compiled artifacts under Configuration/ to a consistent esbuild output format with three structural changes:\n\n1. Hoist static `import` statements above the `__defProp`/`__name` helper shim declarations so every module reads as imports-first, helpers-second, code-third — matching the ideal ESM preamble order.\n\n2. Convert indentation back to tabs from the 2-space style applied in the previous normalize pass; tabs produce narrower diffs against upstream VS Code sources and match the Rust-side fmt convention adopted across the Land project.\n\n3. Normalise array literal formatting, trailing commas, and export blocks — multi-entry arrays get one entry per line with consistent trailing punctuation, and `export { ... }` blocks collapse to single-line when space permits.\n\nAffects all 100+ files across Apply, ESBuild, Plugin, Polyfill, Service, and Transform configuration directories. No runtime behaviour changes.",
			"SHA": "88983f834cd9a4d9dc192f5e596c70ad9a17f2ff"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-20T06:37:16Z",
			"File": [
				"⡟⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				751,
				114
			],
			"Message": "style(Output): Normalize esbuild output formatting across Configuration\n\nConsolidate the compiled JavaScript artifacts under Configuration/ to a uniform code style produced by the esbuild pipeline. Every file is affected by three mechanical changes:\n\n1. Hoist the `__defProp`/`__name` helper shim declarations above all static import statements so the module preamble reads consistently regardless of import count.\n2. Convert indentation from tab-based to 2-space throughout — object literals, array entries, callback bodies, and case clauses now use the same two-space indent as the `Source/` TypeScript originals.\n3. Strip trailing commas from final array/object entries and normalise export blocks to a multi-line `export { ... };` shape.\n\nNo runtime behaviour changes; the transforms (Apply, Copy, Polyfill, Transform, Replace, Rewrite, Strip, Inject, Patch, Instrument) and polyfill shims (Process, FileSystem, IPC, NativeModule, ChildProcess, SharedProcess) are semantically identical before and after.",
			"SHA": "a0b1315b674d1171f639d41ab235149675edf5ec"
		},
